            "text": "At the edge of the town, where the railway became a silver thread between fields, there stood a library no map admitted existed. Its windows were always lit, and its door opened only for people carrying a question they had not yet learned how to ask.\n\nMara found it on a Tuesday after missing her train. She was tired of being sensible, tired of clocks and schedules and the apologies that filled adult life. In her pocket she carried a pebble from the river. When she touched the library door, it swung inward.\n\nInside, shelves climbed beyond sight. Books fluttered their pages like resting birds. A librarian sat behind a desk, knitting something blue.\n\n\u201cWhat are you looking for?\u201d he asked.\n\nMara almost said, \u201cNothing.\u201d Instead, she placed the pebble on the desk.\n\nThe librarian nodded, as though this answered everything. He led her to a narrow staircase and gave her a book with no title. Its first page described the river near her house. The second described the day her father taught her to skip stones. The third was blank.\n\n\u201cYou must write carefully,\u201d the librarian said. \u201cThe unwritten pages are not predictions. They are invitations.\u201d\n\nMara returned home after dark, carrying the book beneath her coat. The next morning, she missed the train again, deliberately. She walked to the river, chose a flat stone, and threw it hard enough to make three bright circles on the water.\n\nThen she opened the book and wrote: Today, I began."
